      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Grayville sat at the end of an oxbow bend of the Wabash River, but a 1985 flood moved the river two miles away, leaving Bonpas Creek as waterway.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        14,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grayville%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Unlike many Illinois oil towns, Grayville’s oil boom in the early 20th century helped it maintain a steady population even after drilling slowed.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        11,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grayville%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A massive flood in January 2005 caused a large rail bridge over the Wabash to collapse, severing the town’s historic rail link to Indiana.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grayville%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Grayville’s early settlers prized its natural port on the Wabash, perched on hills where Bonpas Creek met the river’s deep hairpin bend.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grayville-il.gov/grayville-illinois-history/",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Grayville is the birthplace of naval hero James Meredith Helm, a Medal of Honor recipient from the Civil War.",
